Subject: mtd: rawnand: meson: add Meson8 / Meson8b support

Meson8 (S802), Meson8b (S805) and Meson8m2 (S812) carry the same NAND
flash controller as GXL. Every register offset matches, and so does
every command bit this driver emits, including the M8-specific BCH
numbering: NFC_ECC_BCH8_512=1 .. NFC_ECC_BCH60_1K=7 here is byte for
byte the CONFIG_NAND_AML_M8 branch of Amlogic's own driver
(drivers/amlnf/include/hw_ctrl.h). Nothing in the command path needs to
change.

NONE OF THIS HAS BEEN RUN ON MESON8-FAMILY SILICON. Mainline meson-nand
has never been reported working on any of these SoCs, no Meson8b board
has ever carried an NFC node, and the author of this patch has no such
hardware. Everything below is derived from Amlogic's own vendor sources
and from the clock and pinctrl drivers already in mainline; it is not a
report of anything observed. The next patch makes that operationally
true as well by keeping the MTD read-only by default on this family.

What differs from GXL is everything around the controller, so this turns
the single-field struct meson_nfc_data into a real per-SoC description:

  uses_emmc_clk_reg  GXL and AXG have no NAND clock of their own and
                     divide SD_EMMC_C's instead, which is why probe maps
                     a second "emmc" register and clk_init builds a
                     private divider on it. Meson8b has the real thing:
                     nand_clk_sel -> nand_clk_div -> nand_clk_gate on
                     HHI_NAND_CLK_CNTL, already modelled in
                     drivers/clk/meson/meson8b.c, all three carrying
                     CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT. So "device" is already the
                     rate-settable clock and there is nothing to map.

                     Pointing an "emmc" reg at HHI_NAND_CLK_CNTL instead
                     would be actively harmful: the raw
                     BIT(31)|BIT(24)|BIT(6) write means divider 64 and
                     "NAND clock gate off" in that register's layout,
                     and it would desynchronise clk_regmap's cache.

  soft_waitrdy       Amlogic's m8 driver sets NAND_CTRL_NONE_RB
                     unconditionally, compiles the R/B autodetect out and
                     leaves the request_irq() commented out
                     (drivers/amlnf/phy/hw_controller.c, phy/chip.c), so
                     the interrupt path has never run on this silicon and
                     no device tree carries a number for it. Poll with
                     nand_soft_waitrdy() instead, make the interrupt
                     optional in probe, and do not even request it when
                     the driver is going to poll: a handler on a line
                     nothing arms is a liability, not a fallback.
                     meson_nand.use_irq_rb=1 exists so somebody with the
                     hardware can add an interrupts property locally and
                     find out what the number is.

  always_scramble    hw_controller.c sets controller->ran_mode = 1 for
                     every page it ever writes. Pages the bootloader
                     wrote therefore only read back as anything but noise
                     if the scrambler is on here too, and there is no DT
                     property and no nand_base quirk that can say so.

  boot_page_bch      The short/boot-page mode is hardcoded to
                     NFC_ECC_BCH8_1K, which is the GXL ROM's choice. The
                     M8 ROM uses BCH60_1K (hw_controller.c maps
                     NAND_ECC_BCH_SHORT to NAND_ECC_BCH60_1K).

  new_oob_over_4k    For a page larger than 4KiB the vendor code puts the
                     controller into its "new OOB" mode -- NFC_REG_CFG
                     bits 26:27, info buffer shifted by two entries, OOB
                     bytes as one flat 16-byte block at the front
                     (hw_controller.c, guarded by AML_NAND_NEW_OOB and
                     CONFIG_NAND_AML_M8). This driver models none of it
                     and meson_nfc_select_chip() actively clears those
                     CFG bits, so every vendor-written page on such a
                     part would come back with wrong OOB and an ECC
                     status read from the wrong words. Refuse to attach
                     rather than return plausible rubbish.

  experimental       Consumed by the next patch.

Two things are deliberately NOT per-SoC.

The NFC round-trip delay stays at NFC_DEFAULT_DELAY. The obvious
candidate for an M8-specific value, NAND_CYCLE_DELAY, exists three times
in Amlogic's tree with three different values -- 84 in
drivers/amlnf/include/hw_ctrl.h, 90 in
arch/arm/include/asm/arch-m8b/aml_nand.h, 100 in
arch/arm/include/asm/arch-m8b/nand.h -- and the same trio appears
verbatim for m6, m8, m8b, g9b and g9tv, so none of them is m8b-specific.
The 84 is inside code the M8 build never executes: both of its users in
hw_controller.c sit in `#if 0` blocks and the live path hardcodes
bus_cycle and bus_timing instead. A previous version of this patch used
8400ps on the strength of that constant; on a chip at tRC_min = 20ns
that moves the RE sample point two NFC clocks later than the GXL figure
would, which on a 250MHz bus is most of the data-valid window. Keep the
value that is known to work somewhere until somebody measures this one.

The raw scrambler stays symmetric, which does NOT match the vendor, and
that is deliberate. Amlogic's controller_dma_write() issues raw writes as
NFC_SEND_CMD_M2N_RAW(0, len) -- scrambler hard-off -- while
controller_dma_read() issues raw reads as
NFC_SEND_CMD_N2M_RAW(controller->ran_mode, len) with ran_mode = 1, and
the OOB tail fill three lines below the raw write uses ran_mode as well.
So the vendor's own raw path does not round-trip and contradicts itself
inside one function; copying it would give this driver a raw write whose
data its own raw read cannot recover, which breaks nandwrite --raw
followed by nanddump --raw, and it would not make a restore work either
(a vendor raw read returns descrambled bytes, so writing them back
unscrambled does not reproduce what was on the chip). Recording it here
instead: MTD_OPS_RAW writes through this driver are self-consistent but
are not byte-compatible with what Amlogic's ECC-NONE write path puts on
the flash.

Separately, fix a bug the meson8b clock tree makes reachable.
meson_nfc_setup_interface() computed meson_chip->clk_rate as
1000000000 / div and assumed it would be granted exactly. That holds on
GXL and AXG, where the driver pins the divider's parent to a fixed 1GHz
and registers a plain divider. It does not hold on Meson8b, where
nand_clk_sel and nand_clk_div both carry ROUND_CLOSEST and are therefore
allowed to settle above the requested rate -- i.e. to run the bus faster
than the chip's tRC_min, with no error from any clk API and no symptom
except intermittent uncorrectable ECC. Ask the clock what it will
actually give, refuse anything faster than requested, and re-derive the
nanoseconds-per-clock the rest of the function assumes from the answer.
On GXL and AXG clk_round_rate() returns the request unchanged and every
derived value is bit-identical to before.

pinctrl-meson8b.c already carries the complete "nand" function and
include/dt-bindings/clock/meson8b-clkc.h already exposes CLKID_NAND,
CLKID_NAND_SEL/DIV/CLK, so no other subsystem needs touching.

Also print one geometry/ECC/clock line at attach time. On a board whose
flash is a house-marked die of unknown part number this is the only way
to tell from a boot log whether the clock, the pinmux and the ECC choice
came out right.

